WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.805
A transfer moves 50 from balance a to balance b, in two lines.

2
00:00:07.805 --> 00:00:08.958
Take 50 off a.

3
00:00:08.958 --> 00:00:10.357
Then add 50 to b.

4
00:00:12.033 --> 00:00:15.518
Right then, an auditor thread adds up both balances.

5
00:00:15.518 --> 00:00:17.060
What total does it see?

6
00:00:19.633 --> 00:00:22.204
150, a total that never existed.

7
00:00:22.204 --> 00:00:25.577
Then the credit lands, and it's 200 again.

8
00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:28.424
The fix: one thread at a time.

9
00:00:28.424 --> 00:00:32.721
Every Java object has a built-in lock for this, called its monitor.

10
00:00:32.721 --> 00:00:35.864
Picture a room around the balances, with one key.

11
00:00:36.067 --> 00:00:38.595
synchronized takes the key on the way in.

12
00:00:38.595 --> 00:00:42.480
It hangs the key back on the way out, even if the block throws.

13
00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:46.184
Thread one takes the key, goes in, and runs the debit.

14
00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:48.046
Now the auditor locks too.

15
00:00:48.046 --> 00:00:51.213
The key is gone, so it waits at the door, BLOCKED.

16
00:00:51.213 --> 00:00:52.796
So does another transfer.

17
00:00:52.796 --> 00:00:55.899
The waiting threads form the monitor's entry set.

18
00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:57.964
Thread one is halfway through.

19
00:00:57.964 --> 00:00:59.889
What total can the auditor see?

20
00:01:02.467 --> 00:01:03.068
Only 200.

21
00:01:03.068 --> 00:01:05.808
Thread one finishes and hangs up the key.

22
00:01:05.808 --> 00:01:09.550
Then one waiter gets it, and Java doesn't promise which.

23
00:01:09.550 --> 00:01:12.691
Here it's the auditor, and both lines are done.

24
00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:15.464
The key carries a second promise.

25
00:01:15.464 --> 00:01:18.778
The auditor still has an old copy of a in a register.

26
00:01:18.778 --> 00:01:24.407
Taking the key rules it out: everything thread one wrote before its unlock is visible now.

27
00:01:24.633 --> 00:01:29.557
The memory model calls this a happens-before edge, from the unlock to the next lock.

28
00:01:29.557 --> 00:01:33.543
The auditor at the start skipped the key, so it got neither promise.

29
00:01:34.967 --> 00:01:35.678
Last rule.

30
00:01:35.678 --> 00:01:41.087
Thread one holds the key in withdraw, which calls canCover on the same lock.

31
00:01:41.087 --> 00:01:42.795
Does it wait for itself?

32
00:01:45.367 --> 00:01:45.588
No.

33
00:01:45.588 --> 00:01:47.283
Monitors are reentrant.

34
00:01:47.283 --> 00:01:51.853
The JVM keeps a hold count: two inside canCover, one after it.

35
00:01:51.853 --> 00:01:56.423
When withdraw exits, the count is zero, and the key goes back.

36
00:01:57.233 --> 00:02:00.695
In code, the key is a private final lock object.

37
00:02:00.695 --> 00:02:07.764
Java and Kotlin both compile synchronized to the same two bytecodes: monitorenter and monitorexit.

38
00:02:08.567 --> 00:02:12.810
So give shared state one key, and make every reader and writer take it.

39
00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:17.113
Each unlock hands its writes to the next thread that takes the same key.

40
00:02:17.333 --> 00:02:18.144
Quick check.

41
00:02:18.144 --> 00:02:22.872
Two threads update one balance, but each one locks its own new Object.

42
00:02:22.872 --> 00:02:24.628
Does either one ever wait?
